    "advanced_content_jd3gcQ": {
      "type": "advanced-content",
      "blocks": {
        "liquid_ECbxFi": {
          "type": "liquid",
          "settings": {
            "code": "<h3>LEARN HOW TO INCREASE SPINAL STABILITY, MOBILITY, ENDURANCE, AND WELL-BEING\n</h3><p>Experts estimate that up to <strong>80 percent</strong> of the population will experience back pain at some time in their lives.</p>\n<p>\n\n<strong>It doesn’t need to be this way</strong>.<em> Your Spine, Your Yoga: The Course</em> with Bernie Clark and Dr. Stuart McGill reveals how yoga and other movement practices can be applied in a way that respects our anatomical differences and optimizes back health. </p>\n\n<p>This eight-hour course will guide you in developing a practice unique to your life and your body. It’s for those who experience back pain, yoga teachers and therapists, and movement enthusiasts from all walks of life who want to learn more about the function and mechanics of their spine.</p>\n\n<h3>SPINE STABILITY: THE CORE OF PHYSICAL WELL-BEING</h3>\n<p>The spine is a multi-faceted, multi-functional construct uniquely designed to provide both stability and mobility through our torso to our limbs. While physical yoga practices have been used for decades to increase the range of motion of the spine, the spine’s primary function is, first and foremost, to provide stability through the core of our body. Building this stability is protective for the lower back and prepares the body to endure (and adapt to) multiple forms and levels of stress.</p>",
            "width": "75%",
            "alignment": "center"
          }
        },
        "image_BdtMTY": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/Stu_MultiScreen-1e45f42ca251bc7e8ccf9dce0b3d054c.png",
            "link": "",
            "width": "66%",
            "alignment": "center"
          }
        },
        "liquid_dNncr3": {
          "type": "liquid",
          "settings": {
            "code": "Equally important in developing and maintaining spinal health is understanding how your own uniqueness dictates the nature of your practice. No one has your biology or biography, thus a standardized practice offered to every body is rarely the most effective way to work with your spine.",
            "width": "75%",
            "alignment": "center"
          }
        }
      },
